WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.039
A flash sale has one iPhone

00:00:02.039 --> 00:00:04.174
left. Two shoppers tap Place Order

00:00:04.174 --> 00:00:06.161
on different checkout servers.

00:00:06.161 --> 00:00:07.854
Both see stock equal to one.

00:00:07.854 --> 00:00:08.959
Who gets the item?

00:00:09.189 --> 00:00:10.924
Without concurrency control,

00:00:10.924 --> 00:00:12.927
both requests pass the stock check.

00:00:12.927 --> 00:00:14.462
The database may show zero,

00:00:14.462 --> 00:00:16.664
but two reservations have already been

00:00:16.664 --> 00:00:18.400
promised. That is an oversell.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:20.196
With pessimistic locking,

00:00:20.196 --> 00:00:22.512
transaction A selects the inventory row

00:00:22.512 --> 00:00:24.555
for update. Transaction B waits for

00:00:24.555 --> 00:00:26.530
the lock. No external payment call

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.505
belongs inside this transaction.

00:00:28.735 --> 00:00:30.774
A reserves the item and commits.

00:00:30.774 --> 00:00:32.586
B then reads the updated row,

00:00:32.586 --> 00:00:34.021
finds zero available,

00:00:34.021 --> 00:00:35.380
and returns sold out.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.570
The critical section stays short.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.392
With optimistic locking,

00:00:39.392 --> 00:00:41.709
both transactions read version five.

00:00:41.709 --> 00:00:44.098
A updates successfully to version six.

00:00:44.098 --> 00:00:46.124
B attempts to write version five,

00:00:46.124 --> 00:00:47.789
and its update is rejected.

00:00:48.019 --> 00:00:50.771
The losing optimistic transaction rolls back.

00:00:50.771 --> 00:00:52.698
A new transaction reloads stock.

00:00:52.698 --> 00:00:53.936
With zero remaining,

00:00:53.936 --> 00:00:56.069
the correct business outcome is sold

00:00:56.069 --> 00:00:57.652
out, not repeated retries.

00:00:57.882 --> 00:00:59.488
For a single stock counter,

00:00:59.488 --> 00:01:01.095
use one guarded SQL update:

00:01:01.095 --> 00:01:03.260
decrement only where available is at

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.076
least the requested quantity.

00:01:05.076 --> 00:01:08.149
PostgreSQL rechecks the condition after competing

00:01:08.149 --> 00:01:08.638
writes.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.521
Choose based on contention.

00:01:10.521 --> 00:01:12.862
Keep idempotency, reservation expiry,

00:01:12.862 --> 00:01:14.790
consistent database ownership,

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.374
and order reconciliation.

00:01:16.374 --> 00:01:18.234
Test concurrent transactions,

00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:19.404
monitor lock waits,

00:01:19.404 --> 00:01:21.952
and never confuse cached availability with

00:01:21.952 --> 00:01:22.847
a reservation.

